": "{\"endpoint\": \"create_event\"}", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-a4758e8595fb83b2", "invalid": false, "name": "get_api_docs", "response": {"body": {"description": "Create a calendar event.", "endpoint": "create_event", "error_format": "Errors return {status, error: {type, code, message, param?, hint?}}.", "parameters": {"additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"attendees": {"description": "Attendee email addresses. Look up a contact's email with list_contacts.", "items": {"type": "string"}, "type": "array"}, "end": {"description": "End time. Format: date-time as 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM' (24h, UTC).", "type": "string"}, "location": {"description": "Location.", "type": "string"}, "start": {"description": "Start time. Format: date-time as 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM' (24h, UTC).", "type": "string"}, "summary": {"description": "Event title.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["summary", "start", "end"], "type": "object"}, "returns": "Event object {id, title, start, end, attendees, location, status}."}, "status": 200}, "turn": 3}, {"arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Design Review\", \"start\": \"2026-10-09T13:30\", \"end\": \"2026-10-09T14:15\", \"attendees\": [\"omar.haddad@globex.com\"]}", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-87831e59356e76f7", "invalid": true, "name": "create_event", "response": {"error": {"code": "parameter_invalid_format", "hint": "Example: '2026-10-01 14:00'.", "message": "Invalid format for 'start': expected date-time as 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM' (24h, UTC).", "param": "start", "type": "invalid_request_error"}, "status": 400}, "turn": 4}, {"arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Design Review\", \"start\": \"2026-10-09 13:30\", \"end\": \"2026-10-09 14:15\", \"attendees\": [\"omar.haddad@globex.com\"]}", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-88802eabc3941b79", "invalid": false, "name": "create_event", "response": {"body": {"attendees": ["omar.haddad@globex.com"], "end": "2026-10-09 14:15", "id": "evt_b525f97055", "location": null, "start": "2026-10-09 13:30", "status": "confirmed", "title": "Design Review"}, "status": 201}, "turn": 5}], "episode_id": "calendar.create_meeting.00__rename_param__s0__sft-sample", "final_answer": "The event \"Design Review\" with Omar Haddad has been successfully scheduled for October 9, 2026, from 13:30 to 14:15 UTC. The event ID is `evt_b525f97055`. \n\nNo further actions are required. The meeting is confirmed and ready for participants.", "invalid_calls": 2, "max_turns": 8, "messages": [{"content": "You are an operations assistant with access to the calendar API through tools. Complete the user's request by calling tools. Tool results are JSON with an HTTP-style status. Never guess resource IDs or email addresses: if the request does not give one, look it up with the API's list or search endpoints. If you are unsure how an endpoint works, call get_api_docs. When the request is complete, or you cannot make progress, reply with a short final answer and no tool calls.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Schedule a 45-minute meeting titled 'Design Review' with Omar Haddad on 2026-10-09 from 13:30 to 14:15 UTC.", "role": "user"}, {"content": "<think>\nOkay, let's tackle this user request. The user wants to schedule a 45-minute meeting titled 'Design Review' with Omar Haddad on October 9, 2026, from 13:30 to 14:15 UTC.\n\nFirst, I need to check which function to use. The user is asking to create an event, so the create_event function is the right choice. \n\nLooking at the parameters for create_event: title, start, end are required. The user provided all these details. The start time is 13:30 UTC on 2026-10-09, and the end is 14:15 UTC. The duration is 45 minutes, which fits into the start and end times.\n\nThe attendees are Omar Haddad. The user didn't mention any other attendees, so the attendees array should just have his email. But wait, the user didn't provide an email. Oh, right, the function requires attendees to be an array of email addresses. Since the user didn't specify, maybe I need to look up Omar Haddad's email using list_contacts. But the user didn't mention that, so maybe I should assume they have the email or maybe it's a placeholder. Wait, the user didn't provide the email, so I can't proceed without it. Hmm, this is a problem. The function requires the attendees array, but the user didn't provide the email. \n\nWait, maybe the user expects me to know Omar Haddad's email? But the tools include list_contacts, which can be used to find contacts. However, the user hasn't provided any contact information. So maybe I should call list_contacts to find Omar Haddad's email first. But the user hasn't mentioned that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user expects me to proceed without the email, but that's not possible because the function requires the attendees array. So I need to find Omar Haddad's email. \n\nSo the steps would be: \n\n1. Use list_contacts to find Omar Haddad's email. \n2. Then use add_attendee with the event_id and the found email. \n\nBut the user hasn't provided the event_id yet. The event_id is required for create_event, but the user hasn't given it. So first, I need to create the event, but without the event_id. Wait, no. The create_event function doesn't require the event_id, but the parameters are title, start, end, etc. So the user can create the event with the given details, but the attendees need to be added later. \n\nWait, the user hasn't provided the email, so I can't proceed. Therefore, I need to call list_contacts to find Omar Haddad's email. \n\nSo the correct approach is: \n\n- First, call list_contacts to get Omar Haddad's email. \n- Then, use that email to add him to the event. \n- Then create the event with the title, start, end, and the attendee. \n\nBut the user hasn't mentioned the email, so I need to make a tool call to list_contacts. \n\nBut the user's request is to schedule the meeting, so I need to proceed. However, without the email, the attendees array can't be filled. Therefore, the assistant must first find the email. \n\nSo the assistant should call list_contacts, then use the result to add the attendee. \n\nBut the user's query is to schedule the meeting, so the assistant must make the necessary tool calls. \n\nTherefore, the correct steps are: \n\n1. Call list_contacts to find Omar Haddad's email. \n2. Use that email to add him to the event. \n3. Create the event with the title, start, end, and the attendee. \n\nBut the user hasn't provided the email, so the assistant must first get it. \n\nSo the tool call would be to list_contacts, then use the result to add the attendee. \n\nBut the user's initial request is to schedule the meeting, so the assistant must make the necessary tool calls. \n\nTherefore, the assistant should first call list_contacts to get the email, then proceed to create the event with the attendee. \n\nBut the user hasn't provided the email, so the assistant can't proceed without it. Therefore, the assistant must make the list_contacts call first. \n\nSo the tool call would be to list_contacts, and then use the result to add the attendee. \n\nBut the user's request is to schedule the meeting, so the assistant must handle this step by step. \n\nTherefore, the assistant should first call list_contacts to find the email, then use that email to add the attendee, then create the event. \n\nBut the user hasn't provided the email, so the assistant must make the list_contacts call. \n\nSo the first tool call is list_contacts. \n\nThen, once the email is obtained, the assistant can proceed to add the attendee and create the event. \n\nBut the user's original request is to schedule the meeting, so the assistant must make these steps. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is to call list_contacts to get the email, then proceed with the create_event function. \n\nBut the assistant can't make multiple tool calls in a single response. Wait, the instructions say to call one or more functions. So the assistant should first call list_contacts, then use the result to create the event. \n\nBut in the current context, the assistant needs to generate the tool calls. So the first step is to call list_contacts. \n\nSo the tool call would be to list_contacts, then the assistant would get the email, then add it to the event, then create the event. \n\nBut the user's request is to schedule the meeting, so the assistant must make these steps. \n\nTherefore, the assistant's response would include a tool call to list_contacts, then the next steps would be to add the attendee and create the event. \n\nBut the user's initial request is to schedule the meeting, so the assistant must make the necessary tool calls. \n\nSo the first tool call is list_contacts. \n\nBut the assistant can't make multiple tool calls in one response. Wait, the user's instruction says to call one or more functions. So the assistant can make multiple tool calls. \n\nTherefore, the assistant's response would include the list_contacts call first, then the create_event call once the email is obtained. \n\nBut in the current context, the assistant must generate the tool calls. \n\nSo the first tool call is list_contacts. \n\nThen, once the email is obtained, the assistant can proceed to add the attendee and create the event. \n\nBut the user hasn't provided the email, so the assistant must make the list_contacts call. \n\nTherefore, the correct tool call is to list_contacts. \n\nBut the assistant must return the tool calls in the XML tags. \n\nSo the assistant's answer would be:", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"function": {"arguments": "{}", "name": "list_contacts"}, "id": "chatcmpl-tool-848cc3b9ba70045e", "type": "function"}, {"function": {"arguments": "{}", "name": "list_contacts"}, "id": "chatcmpl-tool-867ae7981f567c60", "type": "function"}]}, {"content": "{\"body\": {\"items\": [{\"email\": \"maya.eriksen@globex.com\", \"id\": \"con_4953334346\", \"name\": \"Maya Eriksen\"}, {\"email\": \"ines.dubois@hooli.com\", \"id\": \"con_3508a61f40\", \"name\": \"Ines Dubois\"}, {\"email\": \"hiro.larsen@acme.io\", \"id\": \"con_d