nt ID: `evt_b525f97055`. Let me know if you need to add attendees, set a reminder, or adjust the details!", "invalid_calls": 1, "max_turns": 8, "messages": [{"content": "You are an operations assistant with access to the calendar API through tools. Complete the user's request by calling tools. Tool results are JSON with an HTTP-style status. Never guess resource IDs or email addresses: if the request does not give one, look it up with the API's list or search endpoints. If you are unsure how an endpoint works, call get_api_docs. When the request is complete, or you cannot make progress, reply with a short final answer and no tool calls.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Schedule a 45-minute meeting titled 'Design Review' with Omar Haddad on 2026-10-09 from 13:30 to 14:15 UTC.", "role": "user"}, {"content": "", "reasoning_content": "Okay, let's see. The user wants to schedule a 45-minute meeting titled 'Design Review' with Omar Haddad on 2026-10-09 from 13:30 to 14:15 UTC.\n\nFirst, I need to check the available functions. The create_event function seems appropriate here. The parameters required are title, start, end, and optionally attendees and location. \n\nThe user provided the title, start time (13:30), end time (14:15), and the attendee's name (Omar Haddad). But the function needs the email address. So I need to look up Omar Haddad's email using list_contacts. \n\nWait, the user didn't provide the email, so I should call list_contacts to find Omar Haddad's email. Once I have the email, I can add him as an attendee. \n\nSo the steps are: \n1. Use list_contacts to find Omar Haddad's email.\n2. Use create_event with the title, start, end, and the attendee's email from the list_contacts result.\n\nI need to make sure the dates are in the correct format. The start is 13:30 on 2026-10-09, which is 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM'. The end is 14:15, so that's 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM'.\n\nWait, the create_event function's parameters require start and end in that format. So the start time is 2026-10-09 13:30, end is 2026-10-09 14:15.\n\nSo the function call would be create_event with title 'Design Review', start '2026-10-09 13:30', end '2026-10-09 14:15', and attendees including Omar Haddad's email. \n\nBut since the user didn't provide the email, I need to first get it from list_contacts. So the first tool call is list_contacts to find Omar Haddad's email. Then use that email in create_event.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"function": {"arguments": "{}", "name": "list_contacts"}, "id": "chatcmpl-tool-b26f7535f702839c", "type": "function"}]}, {"content": "{\"body\": {\"items\": [{\"email\": \"maya.eriksen@globex.com\", \"id\": \"con_4953334346\", \"name\": \"Maya Eriksen\"}, {\"email\": \"ines.dubois@hooli.com\", \"id\": \"con_3508a61f40\", \"name\": \"Ines Dubois\"}, {\"email\": \"hiro.larsen@acme.io\", \"id\": \"con_df1d613d66\", \"name\": \"Hiro Larsen\"}, {\"email\": \"omar.haddad@globex.com\", \"id\": \"con_46211696ce\", \"name\": \"Omar Haddad\"}, {\"email\": \"ana.fischer@initech.dev\", \"id\": \"con_bcfadf89f4\", \"name\": \"Ana Fischer\"}, {\"email\": \"rosa.jensen@initech.dev\", \"id\": \"con_383d4c38c4\", \"name\": \"Rosa Jensen\"}, {\"email\": \"kira.ito@acme.io\", \"id\": \"con_c94d00bf86\", \"name\": \"Kira Ito\"}]}, \"status\": 200}", "name": "list_contacts", "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b26f7535f702839c"}, {"content": "", "reasoning_content": "Okay, the user wants to schedule a meeting titled 'Design Review' with Omar Haddad on October 9, 2026, from 13:30 to 14:15 UTC. Let me see how to approach this.\n\nFirst, I need to check if Omar Haddad is already in the contacts. The previous tool call listed contacts, and Omar's email is 'omar.haddad@globex.com'. So, the next step is to create the event using the create_event function.\n\nThe parameters required for create_event are title, start, end, and optionally attendees and location. The user provided the title, start time (13:30 UTC), end time (14:15 UTC), and the attendee's email. The location isn't specified, so I can leave that out.\n\nWait, the start and end times need to be in the correct format. The user mentioned 13:30 to 14:15 UTC. The format is 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM', so the start would be '2026-10-09 13:30' and end '2026-10-09 14:15'.\n\nI should also check if the user wants to add Omar to the attendees. Since the user mentioned scheduling a meeting with him, I need to include his email in the attendees array. The event ID isn't needed here since it's a new event, but the function requires the event_id, which we don't have yet. Wait, no, the create_event function doesn't require the event_id, but the parameters are title, start, end, and attendees. So I can proceed.\n\nSo the arguments for create_event would be:\ntitle: \"Design Review\"\nstart: \"2026-10-09 13:30\"\nend: \"2026-10-09 14:15\"\nattendees: [\"omar.haddad@globex.com\"]\n\nI need to make sure the start and end times are in the correct format. The user specified 13:30 to 14:15, so that's correct. The date is 2026-10-09, which is the day they want.\n\nOnce the event is created, the user might need to confirm it or add reminders. But the current task is to schedule the meeting. So the tool call should be create_event with the provided parameters.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"function": {"arguments": "{\"title\": \"Design Review\", \"start\": \"2026-10-09 13:30\", \"end\": \"2026-10-09 14:15\", \"attendees\": [\"omar.haddad@globex.com\"]}", "name": "create_event"}, "id": "chatcmpl-tool-83ff1ad15a397604", "type": "function"}]}, {"content": "{\"error\": {\"code\": \"parameter_invalid_format\", \"hint\": \"Example: '2026-10-01T14:00:00Z'.\", \"message\": \"Invalid format for 'start': expected ISO 8601 date-time in UTC, e.g. 'YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ'.\", \"param\": \"start\", \"type\": \"invalid_request_error\"}, \"status\": 400}", "name": "create_event", "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-83ff1ad15a397604"}, {"content": "", "reasoning_content": "Okay, let's see. The user tried to schedule a meeting but got an error. The error message says the 'start' parameter is in the wrong format. The original request had \"2026-10-09 13:30\" which is a date and time without the UTC timezone. The API requires ISO 8601 format with the UTC timezone, like \"YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ\".\n\nSo, the user's start time is 13:30 on 2026-10-09. The correct format should be \"2026-10-09T13:30:00Z\". The end time is 14:15, so that would be \"2026-10-09T14:15:00Z\".\n\nI need to call the create_event function again with the corrected start and end times. The attendees are Omar Haddad, so the email is \"omar.haddad@globex.com\". The other parameters like title and location are provided. The location isn't mentioned, so maybe it's optional. The error was specifically about the start time format, so I'll make sure to include the correct format in the arguments.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"function": {"arguments": "{\"title\": \"Design Review\", \"start\": \"2026-10-09T13:30:00Z\", \"end\": \"2026-10-09T14:15:00Z\", \"attendees\": [\"omar.haddad@globex.com\"]}", "name": "create_event"}, "id": "chatcmpl-tool-9770eb71edfa4a4c", "type": "function"}]}, {"content": "{\"body\": {\"attendees\": [\"omar.haddad@globex.com\"], \"end\": \"2026-10-09T14:15:00Z\", \"id\": \"evt_b525f97055\", \"location\": null, \"start\": \"2026-10-09T13:30:00Z\", \"status\": \"confirmed\", \"title\": \"Design Review\"}, \"status\": 201}", "name": "create_event", "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9770eb71edfa4a4c"}, {"content": "The event \"Design Review\" has been successfully scheduled for October 9, 2026, from 13:30 UTC to 14:15 UTC with Omar Haddad. Event ID: `evt_b525f97055`. Let me know if you need to add attendees, set a reminder, or adjust the details!", "reasoning_content": "Okay, let's see. The user wanted to schedule a 45-minute meeting titled 'Design Review' with Omar Haddad on 2026-10-09 from 13:30 to 14:15 UTC.\n\nFirst, I called list_contacts to find Omar's email. The response showed he's listed under 'omar.haddad@globex.com'. Then, I tried creating the event with the initial details, but the start time was invalid because the format wasn't correct. The user's initial attempt used '2026-10-09 13:30' which is in a format that's not ISO 8601. So I corrected that to '2026-10-09T13:30:00Z' to match the required format.\n\nAfter fixing the start time, the event was created successfully with the ID 'evt_b525f97055'. The response indicated it was confirmed. Now, the user might need confirmation or maybe wants to add someone else. But the initial request was just to schedule the meeting, so the main task is done. I should inform the user that the event is scheduled and maybe check if they need anything else.", "role": "assistant"}], "min_turns": 4, "mutation": {"change": "datetime_to_iso8601", "fields": ["start", "end"], "type": "format_change"}, "policy": "sft-sample", "reward": {"invalid_penalty": 0.05, "success": 1.0, "total": 0.95, "turn_penalty": 0.0}, "schema_version": 1, "seed": 0, "state_changed": true, "success": true, "task": {"domain": "calendar", "id": "calendar.create_meeting.00", "instruction": "Schedule a 45-minute meeting titled 'Design Review' with Omar Haddad on 2026-10-09 from 13:30 to 14:15 UTC.", "split": "train", "template": "create_meeting"}, "terminated": "final_answer", "tools": [{"function": {"description": "List events, optionally filtered to one day.", "name": "list_events", "parameters": {"additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"date": {"description": "Only events starting on this day. Format: date as 'YYYY-MM-DD'.", "type": "string"}, "include_cancelled": {"description": "Include cancelled events (default false).", "type": "boolean"}}, "required": [], "type": "object"}}, "type": "function"}, {"function": {"description": "Get one event by ID.", "name": "get_event", "parameters": {"additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"event_id": {"description": "ID of th