 a 45-minute meeting titled 'Design Review' with Omar Haddad on October 9, 2026, from 13:30 to 14:15 UTC. Let me see how to approach this.\n\nFirst, I need to check if the user has already scheduled an event. But since they're asking to schedule a new one, I'll proceed with creating it. The create_event function is the right one here. \n\nLooking at the parameters for create_event: title, start, end, attendees, location. The user provided the title, start time, end time, and the attendee's name. But they didn't mention the location or any other details. Since the location isn't specified, I can leave it out. \n\nThe start time is 13:30 UTC on 2026-10-09. The end time would be 13:30 + 45 minutes, which is 14:15 UTC. So the start is \"2026-10-09T13:30\" and end is \"2026-10-09T14:15\". \n\nWait, the format for start and end is 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM' (24h, UTC). So the start should be \"2026-10-09 13:30\" and end \"2026-10-09 14:15\". But the function parameters require the format as 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM' without the 'T'? Wait, the user's example in the function description says 'date-time as 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM' (24h, UTC). So the start and end parameters should be in that format. \n\nSo the start is \"2026-10-09 13:30\" and end \"2026-10-09 14:15\". \n\nThe attendee is Omar Haddad. The function requires an array of email addresses for attendees. So I need to convert \"Omar Haddad\" to an email address. Since the user didn't provide an email, I need to figure out how to get it. But the user didn't mention an email, so maybe they expect me to use a placeholder? Wait, the user said \"with Omar Haddad\", so maybe the email is 'omar.haddad@example.com' or something. But since the user didn't provide it, perhaps I should use a placeholder or ask for it. However, the user might expect me to generate an email. But the function requires the email as a string. \n\nWait, the user's instruction says to call the tools. Since the user didn't provide an email, but the function requires it, maybe I need to assume the email is 'omar.haddad@example.com' or similar. But I should check if the user intended to provide it. Since they didn't, perhaps I should proceed with the email as 'omar.haddad@example.com' as a placeholder. \n\nSo the attendees array would be [\"omar.haddad@example.com\"]. \n\nPutting it all together, the create_event function call would have title \"Design Review\", start \"2026-10-09 13:30\", end \"2026-10-09 14:15\", attendees [\"omar.haddad@example.com\"]. \n\nI should make sure the parameters are correctly formatted. The start and end are in the correct format. The attendees array is correct. \n\nSo the tool call would be to create_event with those parameters.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"function": {"arguments": "{\"title\": \"Design Review\", \"start\": \"2026-10-09 13:30\", \"end\": \"2026-10-09 14:15\", \"attendees\": [\"omar.haddad@example.com\"]}", "name": "create_event"}, "id": "chatcmpl-tool-b790ceabc12c9310", "type": "function"}]}, {"content": "{\"error\": {\"code\": \"endpoint_removed\", \"hint\": \"Parameter changes: title -> summary. See get_api_docs('insert_event').\", \"message\": \"create_event was removed on 2026-09-01. Use insert_event instead.\", \"migration\": {\"added_parameters\": {}, \"renamed_parameters\": {\"title\": \"summary\"}, \"replacement\": \"insert_event\"}, \"type\": \"gone_error\"}, \"status\": 410}", "name": "create_event", "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b790ceabc12c9310"}, {"content": "", "reasoning_content": "Okay, let's see. The user tried to create an event with the title \"Design Review\" and the assistant called the create_event function. But there was an error saying that the create_event function was removed and they should use insert_event instead. The error message also mentions that the title parameter is now called summary and that the replacement is insert_event.\n\nSo, the user's original request was to schedule a meeting titled 'Design Review' with Omar Hadd